{"subjectId":"c","id":"pointers-and-arrays","title":"Pointers & Arrays","group":"Pointers","order":24,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Arrays and pointers are closely related but <strong>not</strong> the same thing: an array <em>is</em> storage; a pointer <em>refers to</em> storage. The link is decay — an array name converts to a pointer to its first element.</p>\n<div class=\"formula\"><code>a[i]</code> ≡ <code>*(a + i)</code> ≡ <code>*(i + a)</code> ≡ <code>i[a]</code></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};\n    int *p = a;                      /* decay: p = &amp;a[0] */\n\n    printf(\"%d %d %d %d\\n\", a[2], *(a + 2), p[2], 2[a]);   /* all the same */\n    printf(\"sizeof a = %zu, sizeof p = %zu\\n\", sizeof a, sizeof p);\n\n    p++;                             /* a pointer can move... */\n    printf(\"*p = %d\\n\", *p);\n    /* a++;  ...an array cannot: error, array type is not assignable */\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 3 3 3\nsizeof a = 16, sizeof p = 8\n*p = 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Array <code>int a[4]</code></th><th>Pointer <code>int *p</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it is</td><td>4 ints of storage</td><td>8 bytes holding an address</td></tr><tr><td><code>sizeof</code></td><td>16</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Assignable (<code>a = …</code>)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>&amp;x</code></td><td><code>int (*)[4]</code> — pointer to the whole array</td><td><code>int **</code></td></tr><tr><td>Where the data is</td><td>Inside the array itself</td><td>Somewhere else</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-fingerprint\"></i>&amp;a vs a: same address, different types</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[4] = {0};\n    int *first = a;              /* int *      */\n    int (*whole)[4] = &amp;a;        /* int (*)[4] */\n    printf(\"same address: %d\\n\", (void *)first == (void *)whole);\n    printf(\"first + 1 moves %td bytes\\n\", (char *)(first + 1) - (char *)first);\n    printf(\"whole + 1 moves %td bytes\\n\", (char *)(whole + 1) - (char *)whole);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>same address: 1\nfirst + 1 moves 4 bytes\nwhole + 1 moves 16 bytes</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-tree\"></i>Arrays of pointers vs pointer to array</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 1, y = 2, z = 3;\n    int *arr_of_ptrs[3] = {&amp;x, &amp;y, &amp;z};    /* 3 pointers */\n    int grid[2][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}};\n    int (*row)[3] = grid;                  /* pointer to an array of 3 ints */\n\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", *arr_of_ptrs[1], *arr_of_ptrs[2]);\n    row++;                                 /* moves one whole row */\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", (*row)[0], row[0][2]);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2 3\n4 6</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>sizeof inside a function</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — clang flags the misleading sizeof (-Wsizeof-array-argument)</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid f(int arr[10]) {                    /* the 10 is ignored: arr is int * */\n    printf(\"in f: %zu\\n\", sizeof arr);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int data[10];\n    printf(\"in main: %zu\\n\", sizeof data);\n    f(data);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Are arrays and pointers the same in C?</summary><div><p>No. An array is a contiguous block of objects; a pointer is a single variable holding an address. They behave alike because an array expression decays to a pointer to its first element and <code>a[i]</code> is defined via pointer arithmetic. They differ in <code>sizeof</code>, in assignability and in what <code>&amp;</code> produces.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does int (*p)[5] declare, versus int *p[5]?</summary><div><p><code>int (*p)[5]</code> is <em>one</em> pointer to an array of 5 ints (used to walk 2-D arrays row by row). <code>int *p[5]</code> is an <em>array of 5 pointers</em> to int — <code>[]</code> binds tighter than <code>*</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}